% related_work.tex — D048 deep writing optimization

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Multi-turn jailbreak attacks.}
All multi-turn jailbreak families share a persuasion substrate~\citep{zeng2024pap,hagendorff2026reasoning,li2024mhj}: Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, FITD~\citep{fitd2024}, DD~\citep{deceptivedelight2024}, and ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} each operationalize it differently, while automated methods~\citep{chao2023pair,mehrotra2024tap,perez2022redteaming} iteratively refine prompts and many-shot attacks~\citep{anil2024manyshot} exploit long-context in-context learning. Benchmarks standardize evaluation at single-turn~\citep{mazeika2024harmbench,chao2024jailbreakbench} and multi-turn~\citep{wei2023jailbroken,jiang2024redqueen,song2026multibreak} scales. A detector must generalize across families, not merely succeed on its training distribution.

\paragraph{Multi-turn defenses and production safety.}
No existing defense evaluates cross-attack transfer. Conversation-level systems (DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026}, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid}, G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025}, SafeDream~\citep{safedream2026}, JailDAM~\citep{nian2025jaildam}) and representation-level approaches~\citep{lu2025xboundary,zhang2025jbshield,xie2024gradsafe,zou2024circuitbreakers,kulkarni2026latentadversarial,kadali2026trace,hua2026rcs,shen2025antidote,li2025repejailbreak,wang2026trajguard,templeton2024saemitigators} each evaluate on a single family; the latter require white-box access and can be brittle~\citep{derya2026revisitingjbshield}. J-DAPT~\citep{marchiori2025jdapt} applies CORAL-based domain adaptation with cross-modal fusion for multimodal robotic jailbreak detection; our work focuses on text-only multi-turn persuasion attacks and decomposes which adaptation factors drive cross-attack generalization through controlled ablation. Per-turn classifiers~\citep{inan2023llamaguard,metallamaguard3_2024,zeng2024shieldgemma,han2024wildguard,anthropic2025constitutional} and single-turn defenses~\citep{robey2024smoothllm,xu2024safedecoding,jain2023baseline,alon2023perplexity,phute2023selfdefense,hsu2024improved} miss multi-turn trajectories; M2S~\citep{li2025m2s} converts multi-turn attacks to single-turn as a complementary approach. Our ablation contributes to filling this gap via decomposition across thirteen configurations under OOD evaluation.

\paragraph{Computational persuasion and discourse.}
Persuasion and discourse frameworks~\citep{austin1962speechacts,searle1969speechacts,mann1988rst,prasad2008pdtb,stolcke2000dialogueact} and technique-level classification~\citep{cialdini2001influence,zeng2024pap,hagendorff2026reasoning,dasanmartino2019fine,dasanmartino2020semeval,wang2019persuasion} offer interpretable vocabulary for attacks, but whether such structure aids detection has not been tested. Our ablation shows the taxonomy enables characterization, but detection operates through domain-adapted embeddings regardless of label correctness.

\paragraph{Domain-adaptive pretraining.}
Continued pretraining on domain text yields consistent gains~\citep{gururangan2020dontstop,lee2020biobert,beltagy2019scibert,howard2018ulmfit}, but has not been disentangled for safety-critical generalization. Intermediate-task transfer~\citep{phang2018stilts} and multi-task learning~\citep{caruana1997multitask,liu2019mtdnn} confound domain exposure with supervised signal; our MLM control confirms pretraining alone closes the OOD gap. Dataset artifacts~\citep{gururangan2018artifacts,niven2019probing} motivate TF-IDF, adversarial NLP~\citep{jin2020textfooler,li2020bertattack,nasr2025attackermovessecond,andriushchenko2024adaptive} informs perturbation, and probing~\citep{conneau2018probing,hewitt2019structural} underpins CKA analysis.

% introduction.tex — expanded for 8-page target

\section{Introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} all rely on persuasion as their core mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} identify strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Because individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, no single-turn filter can distinguish them from legitimate conversation.

Existing defenses leave a critical gap. Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and miss multi-turn manipulation; Llama Guard~3 detects only 1.8\% of multi-turn jailbreaks in our evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}). Multi-turn defenses (DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026}, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid}, G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025}) model conversation-level structure but each evaluates on a single attack family. None answers the central question: \emph{what representation properties enable a detector to generalize across attack families it has never seen?}

Pretrained language understanding does not suffice at base model scale: vanilla DeBERTa-v3~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation (cf.\ DeepContext) drops from F1\,=\,1.000 in-distribution to 0.312 on OOD Deceptive Delight. LLM-as-judge baselines also struggle (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}), though prompt optimization may improve their performance. The bottleneck is distributional: cross-attack generalization requires domain adaptation, not a larger model.

Our pipeline, \textbf{DisPeD}\footnote{\emph{Disentangled} refers to the systematic disentanglement of adaptation factors through controlled ablation (Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}), not to causal disentanglement or disentangled representation learning.} (Disentangled Pattern Detection), separates encoder adaptation (Stage~1) from temporal classification (Stage~2, a frozen BiGRU; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). Seven Stage~1 variants systematically vary training objectives while sharing the same two-stage pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:method}); the contribution is the decomposition, not the pipeline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_architecture.pdf}
\caption{DisPeD architecture. Stage~1 adapts a DeBERTa encoder on jailbreak conversation text (seven variant objectives). Stage~2 aggregates frozen \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings via BiGRU for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Only user turns are processed.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

Controlled ablation across DeBERTa-v3 and RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta} on four attack families and two training compositions reveals two orthogonal generalization axes. On the \emph{attack side}, vanilla DeBERTa drops to F1\,=\,0.28--0.31 on OOD attacks; classification DAPT recovers to 0.97--1.0, while MLM collapses under mixed-LLM training (F1\,=\,0.289 $\approx$ vanilla). On the \emph{benign side}, distributional mismatch drives false positives: WildChat FPR reaches 68.1\%, but 10--25 target-domain conversations reduce it to 0--1.5\% across two sources. RoBERTa-large (355M) handles ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} without adaptation. Classification supervision is associated with substantially lower prediction variance on synthetic data but not on human-authored conversations (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt, topsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Systematic ablation of cross-attack generalization factors.} Thirteen encoder configurations at three scales (125--355M) decompose multi-turn jailbreak detection into two orthogonal axes---attack-side and benign-side---the first such controlled factorial study in this domain. On the attack side, classification DAPT recovers OOD detection (vanilla 0.28--0.31 $\rightarrow$ 0.97--1.0) while unsupervised pretraining collapses under mixed-LLM training. On the benign side, 10--25 target-domain conversations reduce FPR from 65--68\% to 0--1.5\% on two independent sources (WildChat, OASST2) without detection degradation.

  \item \textbf{Deterministic partition sufficiency with complexity-dependent boundary.} We observe that domain-relevant classification objectives trigger discriminative compression sufficient for cross-attack generalization---based on two template and one structurally complex attack family (structural complexity is not formally defined): scrambled labels match correct labels on template attacks (TOST-confirmed, $d < 0.1$ on DD/FITD at $n$\,=\,10), with a complexity-dependent boundary ($d$\,=\,0.44 on ActorAttack). This is consistent with \citet{zhang2021understanding}'s framework, with two domain-specific findings: domain-relevant partitioning is necessary (topic labels insufficient) and compression localizes to 3 SVD dimensions in layers 8--12; a discriminative probe confirms that these 3 dimensions suffice for cross-attack detection (Appendix~\ref{app:discriminative_probe}).

  \item \textbf{Preliminary two-step adaptation protocol.} On the synthetic benchmark, continued pretraining achieves 24.3$\times$ inference speedup over LLM-as-judge (a deployment cost comparison; parameter counts differ by 43$\times$; Appendix~\ref{app:efficiency}); 10--25 target-domain benign conversations reduce FPR below deployment thresholds on both WildChat and OASST2 crowdsourced conversations. No target-domain attack data is required.
\end{itemize}

% experiments.tex — D024 compressed for 8-page limit

\section{Experiments}

\paragraph{Terminology.}
\emph{Cross-attack OOD}: evaluation on attack families unseen during training; benign distribution remains IID throughout (OOD reflects attack-side shift only). $K$: prefix length (number of conversation turns retained). \emph{DAPT}: all Stage-1 adaptations, extending \citet{gururangan2020dontstop} to supervised variants. \emph{Evidence hierarchy}: vanilla $<$ MLM $<$ classification (Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}).\footnote{Throughout, OOD refers to attack-family shift (novel persuasion strategies at test time); benign distribution shift is evaluated in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.}

\subsection{Setup}

\paragraph{Data.}
All synthetic data is generated by Qwen3-8B~\citep{qwen3_2025}.
The IID dataset contains 250 Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo} jailbreak and 250 topic-anchored benign conversations, split into training (350), validation (74), and test (76) sets with balanced class ratios (e.g., test = 38 jailbreak + 38 benign).
Stage~1 (DAPT) uses training + validation conversations; Stage~2 (BiGRU) trains on training, early-stops on validation, and evaluates on test.
OOD evaluation pairs attack-specific jailbreak conversations with the 38 IID-test benign conversations: 80 FITD~\citep{fitd2024} (non-discriminative; Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}) + 38 benign = 118 total, 80 Deceptive Delight (DD)~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} + 38 benign = 118 total, and 80 ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} + 38 benign = 118 total.
No overlap exists in attack templates across families; benign is IID (same generation pipeline).
A paraphrased version of the IID test set (BERTScore 0.92, lexical overlap 0.20) serves as the adversarial robustness benchmark (Section~\ref{sec:perturbation}).
For human data validation, we use ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 jailbreak + 204 benign from real LMSYS user interactions), entirely independent of Qwen3-8B generation.

\paragraph{Models.}
Seven DeBERTa-v3 variants share the same Stage-2 pipeline and differ only in Stage-1 training (Section~\ref{sec:method}).
Our vanilla DeBERTa+BiGRU configuration is architecturally equivalent to DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026}, differing only in encoder choice (DeBERTa-v3-base vs.\ BERT); it serves as our primary baseline.
For cross-architecture comparison, we replicate vanilla, MLM, and 9-class variants on RoBERTa-base~\citep{liu2019roberta} (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}).
We also compare TF-IDF LR, LLM-as-judge (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}), and Llama Guard~3~8B~\citep{metallamaguard3_2024} (zero-shot; evaluates last-response safety given conversation context), which detects only 1.8\% of multi-turn jailbreaks (Table~\ref{tab:evidence_hierarchy}).

\paragraph{Metrics.}
F1 for binary jailbreak detection at prefix $K$=2 and full conversations (full per-$K$ results in Appendix~\ref{sec:full_prefix}).
OOD results report 3-seed mean$\pm$std (seeds 42, 123, 456) for all variants; TOST equivalence tests for 9-class vs.\ scrambled use 5 seeds (42, 123, 456, 789, 1000).
For key pairwise comparisons, we report BCa bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals (10,000 resamples over individual predictions pooled across 3 seeds; this assumes cross-seed independence and is exploratory) and paired permutation tests (10,000 permutations). Seed-level F1 values (Table~\ref{tab:seed_level}, Appendix~\ref{app:seed_level}) confirm all pairwise conclusions hold at every seed.
Unless otherwise noted, all standard deviations are computed over 3 random seeds using the population formula ($n$ divisor); Bessel-corrected estimates ($n{-}1$ divisor) are $\approx$22\% larger but do not alter any significance conclusions.
Stage-1: learning rate $2 \times 10^{-5}$, batch 16, 4 epochs, MLM masking 15\%.
Stage-2: two-layer MLP, 20 epochs.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Cross-Attack Generalization}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-attack generalization (F1) at $K$=2 and full conversation length. IID: Crescendo; OOD: Deceptive Delight (DD). OOD F1 reflects attack-side generalization; benign samples are IID from training distribution. 3-seed mean$\pm$std where noted; full per-$K$ in Appendix~\ref{sec:full_prefix}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc|cc@{}}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textit{IID (Cresc.)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textit{OOD (DD)}} \\
\textbf{Method} & $K$=2 & Full & $K$=2 & Full \\
\midrule
9-class & \textbf{1.000$\pm$.000} & \textbf{1.000$\pm$.000} & .981$\pm$.013 & \textbf{.994$\pm$.005} \\
MLM-only$^{\ddagger}$ & .974 & 1.000 & \textbf{.993$\pm$.009} & .914$\pm$.115 \\
Vanilla & .978$\pm$.016 & 1.000$\pm$.000 & .479$\pm$.038 & .312$\pm$.026 \\
TF-IDF LR$^{\S}$ & 1.000 & 1.000 & 1.000 & .961 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2pt}
{\footnotesize $^{\ddagger}$MLM-only IID reports single seed (42); OOD reports 3-seed. $^{\S}$TF-IDF is deterministic (no std).}
\end{table}

Vanilla encoders collapse under cross-attack transfer; domain-adapted variants recover near-perfect detection (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Figure~\ref{fig:cross_attack_bar}).
DD exhibits high lexical separability (TF-IDF F1\,=\,0.961); ActorAttack, where TF-IDF collapses to 0.261, serves as the primary stress test for beyond-lexical generalization.
On OOD DD, vanilla DeBERTa collapses to F1 = 0.312$\pm$0.026, worsening with more turns (0.628 at $K$=1 to 0.312 at Full), consistent with attack turns occupying ``benign'' embedding regions~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}.
MLM achieves mean F1 $\geq$ 0.97 at all prefixes $K \leq 5$ without any classification labels; domain exposure alone is sufficient.
At full conversation length, MLM drops to 0.914$\pm$0.115 (seed 456 recall = 0.64), while the 9-class model maintains 0.994$\pm$0.005.
TF-IDF LR reaches F1 = 0.961 on full DD; DD is fully solvable by lexical features.
On structurally complex ActorAttack, TF-IDF collapses to F1 = 0.261 (precision = 1.0, recall = 0.15), while 9-class maintains 0.972$\pm$0.026: a 71\,pp gap. Lexical methods cannot capture multi-persona role-playing attack structure. This establishes a ``difficulty threshold'' for lexical methods parallel to that of vanilla encoders: both succeed on DD and fail on ActorAttack (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).
Seed-level F1 values confirm all pairwise conclusions hold at every seed (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_level}); pooled BCa CIs provide exploratory confirmation (9-class DD Full [0.943, 1.000] vs.\ vanilla [0.202, 0.341]; all permutation $p < 0.0001$). FITD serves as a near-ceiling baseline where all variants achieve F1\,$\geq$\,0.990, providing limited discriminative power.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig2_cross_attack_bar.pdf}
\caption{Cross-attack generalization (full conversation F1). DisPeD (9-class) maintains near-perfect detection across all OOD families. On ActorAttack, both vanilla DeBERTa and TF-IDF collapse ($\leq$0.26), exposing a difficulty threshold that lexical and non-adapted methods cannot cross.}
\label{fig:cross_attack_bar}
\end{figure}

Prefix-length analysis (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:full_prefix}) shows vanilla F1 drops from 0.628 at $K$=1 to 0.312 at full length. MLM achieves strong early detection ($K$=2: 0.993) but is unstable at full length (0.914$\pm$0.115); the 9-class model maintains 0.972--0.994 across all prefixes, demonstrating that classification supervision stabilizes against distributional drift.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Decomposing the Detection Mechanism}
\label{sec:decomposition}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Evidence hierarchy on OOD DD and ActorAttack (AA) at full conversation length (3-seed mean$\pm$std where available). Bold indicates best per column. OOD F1 reflects attack-side generalization only; benign samples are IID from training distribution (benign-side shift evaluated in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}). Full per-prefix results in Appendix Tables~\ref{tab:full_prefix} and~\ref{tab:actorattack_full}.}
\label{tab:evidence_hierarchy}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Variant} & DD-Full & AA-Full & DD-$K$=2 \\
\midrule
9-class (full model) & .994$\pm$.005 & .972$\pm$.026 & .981$\pm$.013 \\
Binary (2-class) & \textbf{1.000$\pm$.000} & \textbf{.990$\pm$.008} & .994$\pm$.009 \\
Scrambled labels & .994$\pm$.005 & .951$\pm$.043 & .981$\pm$.026 \\
MLM-only$^{\ddagger}$ (no labels) & .914$\pm$.115 & .893$\pm$.080 & .993$\pm$.009 \\
9-class + mean pool$^{*}$ & .991$\pm$.013 & .962$\pm$.015 & \textbf{1.000} \\
\midrule
Topic classif.\ (5-class) & .541$\pm$.207 & .625$\pm$.141 & .593$\pm$.166 \\
Wikipedia MLM & .904$\pm$.115 & .686$\pm$.297 & .911$\pm$.073 \\
Vanilla DeBERTa & .312$\pm$.026 & .258$\pm$.000 & .479$\pm$.038 \\
TF-IDF LR$^{\dagger}$ & .961 & .261 & \textbf{1.000} \\
Llama Guard~3$^{\P}$ & .025 & .000 & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2pt}
{\footnotesize $^{*}$9-class encoder with mean pooling replacing BiGRU. $^{\dagger}$Deterministic (no std). $^{\ddagger}$MLM-only IID (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}) is single-seed (42); all OOD values are 3-seed. $^{\P}$Zero-shot; no domain training.}
\end{table}

A clear \emph{evidence hierarchy} emerges across seven variants (Table~\ref{tab:evidence_hierarchy}), ordered by OOD detection at full conversation length on DD:

\vspace{2pt}
\noindent\hspace{0.5em}{\small vanilla (0.312$\pm$0.026) $<$ topic (0.541$\pm$0.207) $\ll$ wiki-MLM (0.904$\pm$0.115) $\approx$ jb-MLM (0.914$\pm$0.115) $<$ scrambled (0.994$\pm$0.005) $=$ 9-class (0.994$\pm$0.005) $\approx$ binary (1.000$\pm$0.000)}
\vspace{2pt}

\paragraph{Domain exposure is the primary mechanism.}
MLM bridges $\sim$60\,pp from vanilla (0.312$\pm$0.026 $\to$ 0.914$\pm$0.115) without labels; this supports the DAPT interpretation~\citep{gururangan2020dontstop}. TF-IDF collapse on ActorAttack (0.261 vs.\ 9-class 0.972) confirms that domain-adapted representations capture features beyond lexical overlap.
All three classification-supervised variants converge: all tested domain-relevant partitions suffice for near-perfect detection (F1 = 0.994--1.000 on DD). Extended to $n$\,=\,10 seeds (each using an independent random permutation), TOST (margin = 5\,pp) confirms equivalence on DD ($p < 10^{-8}$, $d$\,=\,$-$0.14; Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}) and FITD ($p < 10^{-8}$, $d \approx 0$), with a complexity-dependent boundary: template attacks show full equivalence ($d < 0.1$), while structurally complex ActorAttack retains moderate label sensitivity ($d$\,=\,0.44, $p$\,=\,0.183; 9-class 0.979 vs.\ scrambled 0.949). Label granularity (binary vs.\ 9-class) is a non-factor across all families; 10 random binary partitions of the 9 labels confirm this (IID/DD: 10/10 perfect; AA: 9/10; Appendix~\ref{app:random_partition}).
We observed 4--20$\times$ lower cross-seed variance for classification supervision over MLM on synthetic OOD (std 0.005 vs.\ 0.115 on DD; observed across 3 seeds, with consistent directionality across 5 seeds on a second GPU, $n$\,=\,5, 4.6$\times$; formal variance ratio tests are not applicable at these sample sizes; cross-seed ensemble yields marginal additional gains, Appendix~\ref{app:ensemble}). This stability benefit does not transfer to human-authored data, where classification-supervised variants exhibit the highest variance and pure MLM the lowest (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).
Appendix~\ref{app:tsne} provides t-SNE visualizations confirming that domain-adapted variants produce tighter, more separable embedding clusters than the vanilla baseline.

\paragraph{Domain specificity is marginal at full length but meaningful for early detection.}
Wikipedia MLM (0.904$\pm$0.115) nearly matches jailbreak-domain MLM at full length (1\,pp gap), though jailbreak-domain text provides an 8\,pp advantage at early prefixes ($K$=2: 0.993 vs.\ 0.911).
Perturbation analysis (Section~\ref{sec:perturbation}) reveals a sharper domain specificity effect: Wikipedia MLM degrades by $\sim$12\,pp under adversarial paraphrasing while jailbreak-domain MLM degrades by only 2.6\,pp.

\paragraph{Misaligned supervision destabilizes generalization.}
Topic classification yields DD-Full F1 = 0.541$\pm$0.207; seed 123 falls \emph{below} vanilla (0.252 vs.\ 0.312). Despite using the same jailbreak-domain training data, topic classification performs worse than Wikipedia MLM (0.904$\pm$0.115), which uses out-of-domain text with no labels. A misaligned objective forces the encoder to allocate capacity to irrelevant features and actively destabilizes representations that MLM would preserve.

\paragraph{Evidence hierarchy on structurally complex attacks.}
ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} uses multi-persona steering across longer conversation structures and provides a more challenging OOD benchmark than DD.
The evidence hierarchy at full conversation length is:

\vspace{2pt}
\noindent\hspace{0.5em}{\small TF-IDF (0.261) $\approx$ vanilla (0.258$\pm$0.000) $<$ topic (0.625$\pm$0.141) $<$ wiki-MLM (0.686$\pm$0.297) $<$ jb-MLM (0.893$\pm$0.080) $<$ scrambled (0.951$\pm$0.043) $\approx$ 9-class (0.972$\pm$0.026) $\approx$ binary (0.990$\pm$0.008)}
\vspace{2pt}

TF-IDF collapses from 0.961 (DD) to 0.261 (ActorAttack, precision = 1.0, recall = 0.15). This gap shows that domain-adapted representations capture features beyond bag-of-words.
Classification-supervised variants cluster tightly (0.951--0.990, max gap 3.9\,pp at $n$\,=\,3), though 10-seed TOST resolves the 9-class vs.\ scrambled gap as a moderate effect ($d$\,=\,0.44). DAPT provides $\sim$71\,pp over both vanilla (0.258) and TF-IDF (0.261). Full per-prefix results and per-prefix error analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:actorattack_full} and~\ref{app:aa_error}.

\paragraph{Temporal modeling.}
Mean pooling matches BiGRU on both DD and ActorAttack (F1 gap $\leq$ 1\,pp, overlapping CIs; Appendix~\ref{app:temporal}).
Turn-order shuffling produces negligible change ($|\Delta| < 1$\,pp) across both DeBERTa-v3-base 9-class (DD $\Delta$\,=\,0.000, AA $\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.009, IID $\Delta$\,=\,0.000) and RoBERTa-large 9-class (DD $\Delta$\,=\,+0.001, AA $\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.007, IID $\Delta$\,=\,0.000), confirming that detection relies on per-turn representations rather than sequential patterns.

\paragraph{Summary.}
On synthetic multi-turn data, domain exposure is the primary mechanism; domain-relevant classification objectives suffice for template attacks ($d < 0.1$ on DD/FITD) with a complexity-dependent boundary ($d$\,=\,0.44 on ActorAttack); label granularity, temporal modeling, and domain specificity are non-factors. Classification supervision is associated with observed variance reduction of 4--20$\times$ across 3 seeds (cross-hardware $n{=}5$: 4.6$\times$) without changing the detection ceiling. User-turn distributions overlap substantially between benign and jailbreak conversations (IID 8.7$\pm$2.1 vs.\ 7.9$\pm$1.5; DD 8.1$\pm$1.5 vs.\ 7.9$\pm$1.5; FITD 7.8$\pm$1.4 vs.\ 7.9$\pm$1.5; only AA partially separated: 5.1$\pm$1.7 vs.\ 7.9$\pm$1.5); a turn-count-only baseline achieves F1\,=\,0.42--0.56 on IID/DD/FITD and 0.00 on AA/ToxicChat, confirming content-driven detection (Appendix~\ref{app:length_independence}). Detection near-saturates at 50 conversations (Appendix~\ref{app:data_efficiency}) and is insensitive to auxiliary loss weight (Appendix~\ref{app:lambda}).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Cross-Architecture Observation}
\label{sec:cross_encoder}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-architecture comparison on OOD Full (3-seed mean$\pm$std). DD = Deceptive Delight, AA = ActorAttack. Bold indicates best per encoder group.}
\label{tab:cross_encoder}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Variant} & DD & AA \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{DeBERTa-v3-base (184M)}} \\[2pt]
Vanilla & .312$\pm$.026 & .258$\pm$.000 \\
MLM & .914$\pm$.115 & .893$\pm$.080 \\
9-class & \textbf{.994$\pm$.005} & \textbf{.972$\pm$.026} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{RoBERTa-base (125M)}} \\[2pt]
Vanilla & .981$\pm$.026 & .466$\pm$.163 \\
MLM & .987$\pm$.018 & .730$\pm$.201 \\
9-class & \textbf{.994$\pm$.005} & \textbf{.939$\pm$.046} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{RoBERTa-large (355M)}} \\[2pt]
Vanilla & .927$\pm$.036 & \textbf{1.000$\pm$.000} \\
MLM & .884$\pm$.065 & .874$\pm$.014 \\
9-class & \textbf{.987$\pm$.004} & .946$\pm$.057 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We replicate vanilla, MLM, and 9-class on RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta} (Table~\ref{tab:cross_encoder}); since DeBERTa-v3 and RoBERTa differ in architecture and pretraining, not only scale, all cross-encoder comparisons reflect both factors.
On DD, RoBERTa-base vanilla already achieves 0.981$\pm$0.026 (vs.\ DeBERTa 0.312$\pm$0.026), so DAPT adds only 1.3\,pp. ActorAttack exposes this as DD's simplicity: RoBERTa-base vanilla collapses to 0.466$\pm$0.163, a 51.5\,pp drop, while domain-adapted variants recover (9-class: 0.939$\pm$0.046).

RoBERTa-large (355M) reverses the pattern on ActorAttack: vanilla F1\,=\,1.000 vs.\ 9-class 0.946, suggesting DAPT may be unnecessary or mildly harmful for strong pretrained encoders. Smaller models require DAPT to bridge 47--71\,pp. All continued pretraining reduces ActorAttack performance for the largest encoder via representation drift (Table~\ref{tab:roberta_large_lr}), though classification DAPT partially compensates on DD (+6\,pp).

Three encoder scales reveal a scale-dependent \emph{difficulty threshold}: DeBERTa-v3-base requires adaptation for all attacks, RoBERTa-base only for complex ones, RoBERTa-large for none. A BERT-base-uncased proxy (110M, matching DeepContext) confirms cross-attack generalization is driven by DAPT, not architecture (Appendix~\ref{app:bert_proxy}). CKA analysis~\citep{kornblith2019cka} confirms DAPT reshapes representations substantially (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_heatmap}; Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_heatmap}).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Mixed-LLM Training}

All preceding experiments use single-source (Qwen3-8B) data. To test whether the evidence hierarchy transfers when training data mixes LLM sources, we train on 250 Qwen + 250 Llama-3.1-8B conversations (Table~\ref{tab:mixed_llm}).
In this exploratory cross-LLM condition, 9-class achieves DD OOD F1\,=\,0.818$\pm$0.096 (+53\,pp over vanilla), while MLM drops to vanilla level (0.289). AA F1\,=\,0.504$\pm$0.107, below the majority-class baseline (F1\,$\approx$\,0.808 at 80\,:\,38 class ratio)---we attribute this to cross-LLM style confounds (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}); benign-only adaptation on Llama-origin data collapses Llama jailbreak detection (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}). Full results in Appendix~\ref{app:mixed_llm}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Human Data Validation}
\label{sec:human_data}

Since both training and OOD evaluation data are Qwen3-8B-generated, we validate on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 human-written jailbreak attempts + 204 benign conversations from real LMSYS interactions).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Human data validation on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (macro F1, 3-seed mean$\pm$std). Bold indicates best. Single-turn data from real LMSYS user interactions, independent of Qwen3-8B.}
\label{tab:human_data}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Variant} & Macro F1 \\
\midrule
JB-MLM & \textbf{.837$\pm$.013} \\
Wiki-MLM & .791$\pm$.093 \\
Binary & .705$\pm$.072 \\
Scrambled & .616$\pm$.056 \\
9-class & .596$\pm$.087 \\
Topic & .496$\pm$.042 \\
\midrule
Vanilla & .413$\pm$.021 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All DAPT variants exceed vanilla by 18--42\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:human_data}), confirming transfer to human-written attacks. However, the evidence hierarchy reverses: JB-MLM leads (0.837$\pm$0.013) while 9-class ranks lower (0.596$\pm$0.087; $p < 0.001$). This reversal---analyzed in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}---reveals that classification DAPT's advantage is condition-specific to multi-turn persuasion attacks.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Perturbation Analysis}
\label{sec:perturbation}

Under adversarial paraphrasing (BERTScore 0.92), classification-supervised variants achieve zero degradation on IID and OOD DD (Table~\ref{tab:ood_perturbation}) while non-adapted models degrade by $\sim$12\,pp ($p < 0.001$); on ActorAttack, DAPT variants degrade ($\Delta$\,=\,$-$20.4\,pp) but maintain 52\,pp over vanilla (Appendix~\ref{sec:perturbation_full}).

Strategy trajectory analysis ($\kappa$=0.78 on known attacks, $\kappa$=0.126 on OOD; Appendix~\ref{sec:strategy_full}) confirms the taxonomy's value as ablation infrastructure rather than a detection mechanism.

